 the sort of scale that  Germany had if you look at other similar  countries that start in a same sort of  place like France we've tested for more  people than in France so if you look  internationally there are examples where  they started in a stronger place there's  examples where like us they've been  having to build up from a much smaller  base all that matters to me is getting  this right and getting the the British  national effort on testing up to up to  the scale it needs to be in the same way  that we're expanding the NHS and making  sure that there's always the ventilator  capacity that people need for this very  specific illness so you know we of  course we look internationally to learn  from what's what we can from abroad but  all that really matters is delivering  for the nation and not in the whole  country  coming together to try to solve this  great problem of coronavirus one of the  big problems of course is to do with  staffing in the NHS at the moment and  hopefully testing will obviously  eliminate some of those issues we know  and when it comes to frontline staff  we've had some some high-profile cases  of people who have sadly died as a  result of this where are we in terms of  frontline staff who are critically ill  well they it's true that and the the  testing for frontline staff is a really  important part of what we need testing  for as of yesterday morning five  thousand frontline staff had had been  tested it's also important that we have  tests for the families of critical staff  so that they don't have to isolate if  one of their family becomes ill so they  can get to work and and as of yesterday  that's being rolled out - and the that  you know forgive me for interrupting me  for interrupting but where are we on  where are we on the numbers here because  actually the number  are critical you must be aware of the  numbers because of the direct impact  it's having on frontline services yes  exactly I was just about to come onto  that so